# Professional UI Quality Rule

- Read the Mandatory UI and Responsive-Design Standard in `docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` before changing UI.
- Use the InventoriMan direction: calm, precise, data-forward retail operations software.
- Plan the visual hierarchy and responsive behavior before writing components.
- Reuse design tokens and shared primitives; do not introduce isolated visual systems.
- Keep panels proportionate to their content and avoid oversized empty cards, excessive padding, and deeply nested wrapper `div` elements.
- Prefer semantic structures and focused components with one clear responsibility.
- Keep operational screens compact, scannable, and efficient without making controls cramped.
- Use maximum content widths for forms and settings instead of stretching them unnecessarily.
- Make navigation, tables, charts, filters, forms, and actions usable at every required viewport.
- Do not hide essential actions merely to make a narrow layout fit.
- Include loading, empty, error, offline, disabled, focus, hover, and success states.
- Meet WCAG 2.1 AA, preserve keyboard access, show focus, and respect reduced motion.
- Add interaction tests and responsive visual coverage for meaningful UI changes.
- Inspect screenshots at the plan's required sizes before declaring UI work complete.
- Do not approve UI work with clipping, overlap, unintended page overflow, unreadable charts, inconsistent spacing, or generic template styling.
- Require owner visual acceptance as part of the current phase gate.
